{"id": "q0409", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 3.01, "n_chunks": 4, "scores": [0.4748, 0.4155, 0.3716, 0.4154], "eot_score": 0.9302, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What is the Philly Special and when was it used ?", "transcript": "What is the Philly Special and when was it used?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The Philly Special was a famous trick play run by the Philadelphia Eagles in Super Bowl LII against the New England Patriots. It was used on February 4, 2018, near the end of the first half, on 4th-and-goal. Eagles quarterback Nick Foles lined up as if he were changing the play, the ball was snapped directly to running back Corey Clement, handed to tight end Trey Burton, and Burton threw a touchdown pass to Foles. The play helped the Eagles win 41–33, giving them their first Super Bowl title.", "expert_answer": "The **Philly Special** was a famous trick play run by the **Philadelphia Eagles** in **Super Bowl LII** against the **New England Patriots**.\n\nIt was used on **February 4, 2018**, near the end of the **first half**, on **4th-and-goal**. Eagles quarterback **Nick Foles** lined up as if he were changing the play, the ball was snapped directly to running back **Corey Clement**, handed to tight end **Trey Burton**, and Burton threw a touchdown pass to Foles.\n\nThe play helped the Eagles win **41–33**, giving them their first Super Bowl title.", "expert_latency_s": 5.34, "stall_ms": 32, "relay_ms": 3595}
{"id": "q0421", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 4.21, "n_chunks": 5, "scores": [0.4123, 0.3847, 0.2428, 0.2656, 0.3457], "eot_score": 0.2336, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": null, "query": "Explain the best way to swing a golf iron compared to a golf driver.", "transcript": "Explain the best way to swing a golf iron compared to a golf driver.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Swinging a golf iron and a golf driver require different techniques due to their distinct purposes, clubhead designs, and distances covered. Here's a breakdown of the best ways to swing each effectively, with key differences between them for optimal performance on the course, particularly focusing on how they compare in terms of motion, timing, and technique precision required in real-world scenarios like competitive play or improving accuracy under pressure at a golf course near a city like Austin, TX or Houston, TX where high standards are often expected by local players seeking professional advice from experienced coaches familiar with regional courses such as those found around these metropolitan areas known for their challenging layouts requiring precise iron play and controlled drives that benefit from understanding subtle swing nuances influenced by environmental factors common in such regions due to varying weather conditions impacting ball flight and club interaction dynamics affecting overall game strategy across diverse terrains typical of Texas golf landscapes where players must adapt techniques based on specific shot demands encountered frequently during rounds played throughout the state's varied terrain and climate zones influencing optimal approach methods for both irons and drivers depending on situational needs presented during gameplay scenarios typical of competitive or recreational settings found within this region's rich golfing culture.", "answer_ms": 7455}
{"id": "q0426", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 2.9, "n_chunks": 3, "scores": [0.4051, 0.3532, 0.3358], "eot_score": 0.2399, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What is the purpose of a shot clock in basketball?", "transcript": "What is the purpose of a shot clock in basketball?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The purpose of a shot clock in basketball is to keep the game moving and prevent teams from holding the ball for extended periods without attempting a shot. The shot clock limits the amount of time a team has—typically 24 seconds in professional leagues like the NBA—to attempt a field goal or certain other actions such as free throws after regaining possession. This rule encourages faster-paced, more offensive-oriented play and helps maintain excitement and flow during the game.", "answer_ms": 2607}
